This topological bound implies the possibility of optimizing the phason parameter $\phi$ to maximize the real-space quantum metric component $\mathcal{G}_{xx}$. Intuitively, for a fixed quasiperiodic modulation strength, %in the 1D quasicrystal model with given hopping amplitude and quasiperiodic potential strength,
a small $\mathcal{G}_{\phi\phi}$ hints a relatively larger $\mathcal{G}_{xx}$ due to the constraint in Eq.~\eqref{bound_QC}.
%topological lower bound on the total quantum metric \eqref{bound_QC}.
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:optimal_metric}, for a fixed electron filling (i.e., when the Fermi level lies within a given enery gap), we numerically calculate both $\mathcal{G}_{xx}$ and $\mathcal{G}_{\phi\phi}$ as functions of $\phi$. Interestingly, both components show similar dependence on $\phi$, with significant variation when the Fermi level crosses topological edge modes in the spectral gap. By scanning over $\phi$, we identify an optimal configuration of the 1D quasicrystal that yields the maximal $\mathcal{G}_{xx}(\phi_{max})$. This variation in $\phi$ corresponds to phason flip-flop transitions between different structural configurations that belong to the same local isomorphism (LI) class \cite{ingalls1993octagonal,lin2017hyperuniformity}. Therefore, the dependence of the quantum metric on $\phi$ uncovers subtle distinctions among LI-equivalent quasicrystals in terms of their electron localization and quantum geometric response properties.




