\section{Helicons dispersion relation}
\begin{figure} [t] 
\includegraphics[scale=.5]{omega_vs_k_tilt.png} 
\caption{The $\omega$ vs. k dispersion for multi-WSMs at different values of parameters. The other parameters are taken as $v_F$ = 0.005, $\mu$ = 0.4, $\epsilon_b=5$, $b_z=.01 \pi/3.5$,  B = 3, $b_0=0$ and fine-structure constant $\alpha=1/137$} 
\label{dis_plot}
\end{figure}
Next, we define the dielectric tensor
\begin{eqnarray}
\epsilon_{l m}= \delta_{l m}\epsilon_b + \frac{4\pi i}{\omega}\Bigl[\sigma_{l m}-\epsilon_{l m n}\frac{\alpha c}{2 \pi^2}\bigl(b_n-q_n\frac{b_0}{\omega}\bigr)\Bigr]
\end{eqnarray}

\noindent where $\epsilon_{l m n}$ is the Levi-Civita antisymmetric tensor and the indices l,m and n run over the Cartesian coordinates x,y and z.  The above equation can be combined with the wave equation and gives the following relation.

\begin{eqnarray}
\Bigl(\frac{c k}{\omega}\Bigr)^2-\frac{2\alpha}{\pi c \omega}\Bigl(b_z-\frac{b_0 k}{\omega}\Bigr)=\epsilon_b +\frac{4\pi i}{\omega} (\sigma_{xx}-i \sigma_{xy})
\label{dis_t}
\end{eqnarray}
From the above Eq.(\ref{dis_t}), the dispersion relations have been plotted in Fig.(\ref{dis_plot}). This plot shows how the dispersion changes with the tilt parameter and helicon modes exist with tilting the Weyl semimetals. This is our main results that tilt modify the dispersion relations.  

Next, we will look at the behavior of gapped collective modes at $\bf B=0$. 
The expressions for conductivities tilted along the z-direction have been discussed in the literature
\cite{detassis2017collective}

\begin{eqnarray}
\sigma_{xx}=\frac{e^2 \omega}{12 v_F \pi}\Bigl[1-\theta(t-1)\Bigl(1-\frac{3t^2+1}{4t^3}\Bigr)\Bigr]
\end{eqnarray}
and
\begin{eqnarray}
\sigma_{yy}=\frac{e^2 \omega}{12 v_F \pi}\Bigl[1-\theta(t-1)\Bigl(1-\frac{3t^2-1}{2t^3}\Bigr)\Bigr]
\end{eqnarray}

Note that the above expressions of the conductivities reduce the result of the without tilted case for all values of $t<1$ (type-I WSMs)\cite{kargarian2015theory}. In the long-wavelength limit, we find that three gapped modes $\Omega_{\lambda}(q)$ with $\lambda=1,2,3$ are given by
\begin{eqnarray}
\Omega_{1}(k=0)=\omega_{-}\\
\Omega_{2}(k=0)=\omega_{p}/\sqrt{\epsilon_b}\\
\Omega_{3}(k=0)=\omega_{+}
\end{eqnarray}

\noindent where $\omega_{\pm}=\alpha c b/(\pi \epsilon_b) \pm \sqrt{
(\alpha c b)^2/(\pi \epsilon_b)^2+\omega_{p}^2/\epsilon_b}$ with $b=\mid\bf b\mid$

\noindent where $\omega_{p}$ define the plasma frequencies in WSMs.
\begin{eqnarray}
\omega_{p}^2= \frac{4 e^2 \omega_{c}^2}{3 \pi \hbar v_F}
\end{eqnarray}

Therefore, the degeneracy of the three gapped collective modes at k=0 is lifted by the presence of the axion term in the electromagnetic response of WSMs. \\

\section{Conclusion}
In summary, we have studied helicon modes in type-I WSMs from semiclassical Boltzmann transport theory, with the inclusion of the orbital angular momentum. We have calculated the analytical expressions for the longitudinal part of the conductivity tensor as a function of the tilt parameter. The transverse parts of conductivity as a function of the tilt term have been calculated numerically. The dispersion modes exist in tilted WSMs. The degeneracy of the three gapped collective modes at k=0 is lifted by the presence of the axion term in the electromagnetic response of WSMs, but is unaffected by a tilt parameter for $t<1$. It is very interesting to study the helicon modes for type-II WSMs($t>1$) case.

