\section{Discussion}
\label{sec: Discussion}

\subsection{Symbolic Trees vs. Unified Symbolic Networks}
\label{sec: Symbolic Trees vs. Symbolic Networks}
Symbolic trees represent mathematical expressions hierarchically, leaves represent operands (constants/variables), internal nodes denote operators, and edges define operational dependencies. However, unified symbolic networks represent mathematical expressions through forward propagation: input nodes encode variables, hidden layers apply operations, and edges carry learnable weights. Then we will discuss the differences between them.

\textbf{Linear Transformation:} Symbolic Networks replace the $\{+,-\}$ operators with linear transformations, implying that the $\{+,-\}$ operators are no longer confined to binary operators but can achieve multivariate interactions, thereby adapting to high-dimensional feature spaces. The linear transformations in symbolic networks inherently constitute affine mappings in vector spaces, therefore the expressive capacity of symbolic networks forms a superset encompassing all linear operations achievable by symbolic trees.  
For the $i$-th node in Equation \ref{eq: UniSymNet forward}, linear transformation can be written as:
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq: node forward}
     y_i^{(l)} = \sum_{j=1}^{d_{l-1}} W_{ij}^{(l)}z_j^{(l-1)} + b_i^{(l)}, \quad \forall i=1,2,\cdots,d_l,
\end{equation}
However, for the linear transformation in Equation \ref{eq: node forward}, symbolic tree representation requires constructing a binary tree structure for each $i$-th node. Its minimum depth grows as:
\[
\text{Depth}_{\text{tree}} = \left\lceil \log_2 \left( \sum_{j=1}^{d_{l-1}} \delta_{ij} \right) \right\rceil + 1,
\]
where $\delta_{ij} \in \{0,1\}$ indicates whether the connection $W_{ij}$ exists. 
