\begin{definition}[Set Convergence]\label{def:set-lim}\em
We say that a sequence of sets $\{S_n:n\geq 1\}$ converges to a limit $S$ if as $n\to\infty$ $\limsup_{n\to\infty} S_n=\liminf_{n\to\infty} S_n=S$.
\end{definition}
% It can be shown that $S$ is closed (Proposition 4.3, \cite{rockafellar2009variational}), and that it only depends on the closure of $S_n$'s.
% Examples of sets that converge  are $S_n=B(\xx_n,\rho_n)$ with $(\xx_n,\rho_n)\to (\xx,\rho)$, and bounded, monotone sequences of sets (either increasing or decreasing). 
We refer the reader to \cite[Chapter 4]{rockafellar2009variational} for additional examples and sufficient conditions required for set convergence. The following definition (see \cite{dembo2009large}, Pg. 6) is critical to this the subsequent proofs: 
\begin{definition}[Large Deviations Principle]\label{def:LDP}\em
    We say that a sequence of random vectors $\{\xxi_n:n\geq 1\}$ satisfies a large deviations principle (LDP) with rate function $J$ if as $n\to\infty$ 
    \begin{equation}\label{eqn:LDP}
       \limsup_{n\to\infty} n^{-1}
\log \mathbb P(\xxi_n\in F) \leq -\inf_{\xx\in F} J(\xx) \quad \text{and}\quad \liminf_{n\to\infty}n^{-1}\log \mathbb P(\xxi_n\in G) \geq  -\inf_{\xx\in G} J(\xx) 
    \end{equation}
for every closed set $F$ and open set $G$. We denote this by $\xxi_n\in {\tt LDP}(J)$.    
\end{definition}
% An LDP roughly translates to regularity in the tails of the distribution of $\xxi_n$ as $n\to\infty$. An implication of Definition~\ref{def:LDP} is that for small $\delta$ and sufficiently large $n$, $\mathbb P(\xxi_n \in B(\xx,\delta)) \approx \exp(-n I(\xx))$. Therefore, whenever $\xxi_n\in {\tt LDP}(J)$, the rate function $J(\cdot)$ encodes the "shape" of rare events associated with $\xxi_n$, while $r_n$ gives the "size". Indeed whenever $\xxi_n\in {\tt LDP}(J)$ if $J(\xx_1)<J(\xx_2)$, $\xxi_n$ is orders of magnitude less likely to lie in a neighbourhood around $\xx_2$ than in a neighbourhood around $\xx_1$.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:ldp}
Suppose the density of $\xxi$ satisfies Assumption ($\mv{\mathscr{L}}$). Then $\{\xxi/\lambda^{\leftarrow}(t):t> 0\}\in {\tt LDP}(\varphi^*)$.     
\end{proposition}

