\begin{abstract}
While artificial intelligence (AI) has been promising for fusion control, its inherent black-box nature will make compliant implementation in regulatory environments a challenge. This study implements and validates a real-time AI enabled linear and interpretable control system for successful divertor detachment control with the DIII-D lower divertor camera. Using $D_2$ gas, we demonstrate feedback divertor detachment control with a mean absolute difference of 2\% from the target for both detachment and reattachment. This automatic training and linear processing framework can be extended to any image based diagnostic for regulatory compliant controller necessary for future fusion reactors.
\end{abstract}

\vspace{2pc}
\noindent{\it Keywords}: Machine Learning, Regulatory Compliance, Interpretable AI, Computer Vision, Detachment, Tokamak, DIII-D, Plasma Control

